Subject: [PATCH] Custody v2 panel: correct the SessionVault refusal; prove
 both factory states on the real contracts
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Per root's 20:00-20:04 review:
- With no sessionVaultFactory in the book, SessionVault.ownerCall WOULD satisfy the original vault (msg.sender = the
  SessionVault). The earlier message claiming it would not help was wrong.
- The dashboard still refuses SessionVault-held deployments in that state, for the real reason: a policy created that
  way is pinned to the SessionVault. After the administrator configures the factory it cannot be updated by the wallet or
  the SessionVault, and only claimPolicy (which destroys the bundle) follows. The administrator's factory entry is the gate.
- test/custody-v2-dashboard-contract.test.mjs, on the real vault/book/SessionVault:
  - with no factory: the EOA's direct update reverts NotOwner; ownerCall succeeds and pins the SessionVault;
  - the factory activation is root's exact calldata shape (book.set(sessionVaultFactory, 0x1f5c…));
  - after activation: the dashboard's direct EOA save works; ownerCall and session keys revert NotOwner; the pinned policy
    is stranded (page: owner_moved), and claimPolicy destroys its bundle.
- The SessionVault factory gets a real stand-in ERC-20 (its post-ownerCall balance check).
- docs/security/secret-custody-v2.md:
  - a dated "current proven state" (2026-10-23, root's read-only evidence) beside the preserved Oct-14 record;
  - the ownerCall/factory semantics.
